{"name":"Chamois Tan","hex":"#b3a385","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chamois","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A short-horned goat antelope native to mountainous terrain in southern Europe; Rupicapra rupicapra.","Ellipsis of chamois leather (“soft pliable leather originally made from the skin of chamois (nowadays the hides of deer, sheep, and other species of goat are alternatively used)”)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Chamois-colored."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chamois"},{"term":"tan","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["The trigonometric function tangent."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Tangale."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tan"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chamois","blocks":[{"text":"The chamois, or Alpine chamois, is a species of goat-antelope native to mountainous parts of Europe and Western Asia, from the Pyrenees and the Cantabrian Mountain Range, the Alps, the Apennines, the Dinarides, the Tatra to the Carpathian Mountains, the Balkan Mountains, the Rila–Rhodope massif, Pindus, the northeastern mountains of Turkey, and the Caucasus. It has also been introduced to the South Island of New Zealand. Some subspecies of chamois are strictly protected in the EU under the European Habitats Directive."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/064_Wild_Chamois_Parc_r%C3%A9gional_Chasseral_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/330px-064_Wild_Chamois_Parc_r%C3%A9gional_Chasseral_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chamois","wikidata":"Q131340"},{"title":"Tan (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Tan is a pale tone of brown. The name is derived from tannum used in the tanning of leather."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Chestnut_Oak_Bark.jpg/330px-Chestnut_Oak_Bark.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_camp